# context: explicit context management for a Dagger coding agent.
#
# The premise is that a chat log is a bad place to keep what a project knows.
# It is append-only, unreviewable, unshareable and it rots in silence: the
# model happily "remembers" something that stopped being true three commits
# ago, and the only cure is to start over and re-derive it. Worse, the log is
# where the *thinking* went — the constraints that were weighed, the option
# that was rejected and why — and all of it is discarded when the session ends.
#
# This module keeps that material instead, as ordinary files in the repository:
#
#   context/
#     glossary/…    what the words mean here
#     facts/…       small, checkable claims about the code
#     decisions/…   what was decided and why, once
#     designs/…     where something is going
#
# Four things make that more than a folder of notes an agent could already
# read and write with the editor tools:
#
#   1. COMPOSITION. `docCompose` assembles a bundle from a selection plus the
#      closure of everything it links to, ordered so definitions land before
#      the things that use them, deduplicated, and clipped to a budget with an
#      explicit manifest of what was left out. A session starts by composing
#      the context it needs, rather than accumulating one by accident.
#
#   2. EVIDENCE. A document declares how it is tied to the implementation:
#      `anchors` (the files it describes), `asserts` (literal claims about
#      those files, checked mechanically) and `tests` (the tests that
#      demonstrate it). `docWrite` REFUSES a document whose assertions do not
#      hold, so a fact that does not check out cannot be recorded in the first
#      place.
#
#   3. DRIFT. Each document records `verifiedAt`: the newest commit touching
#      its anchors at the moment it was last reconciled. When the anchored code
#      moves on, the document is drifted, `docStale` names the commits that
#      moved it, and the `context:drift` check goes red until somebody re-reads
#      and re-stamps it. That is the whole convergence loop: docs cannot
#      quietly fall behind the code, because the code changing is what makes
#      them visibly wrong.
#
#   4. COMPOSITION AGAIN, at session start. The @agent middleware folds the
#      corpus index — and any document marked `pin` — into the system prompt,
#      so the model opens every session already knowing what the project knows
#      and what has drifted, without a single message of chat history.
#
# Persistence is the repository itself. Documents are markdown with YAML front
# matter; `docWrite` returns a Workspace, so a write lands in the session's
# changeset exactly like a source edit — it shows up in `status`/`diff`, gets
# committed with the code that motivated it, and is reviewed in the same pull
# request. There is no separate store to sync, and `git log context/` is the
# history of what the project learned.

"""
The parsed front matter of a context document.

Every field is optional in the file; the defaults here are what an absent
field means.
"""
type DocMeta {
  """One-line title. Falls back to the document id when absent."""
  title: String! = ""
  """glossary | fact | decision | design | note"""
  kind: String! = "note"
  """draft | active | superseded"""
  status: String! = "active"
  """Free-form labels, used to select documents for composition."""
  tags: [String!]! = []
  """Repository-relative files or directories this document describes."""
  anchors: [String!]! = []
  """
  Mechanically checkable claims, one per entry:

    "<path> ~ <regexp>"    the file must match
    "<path> !~ <regexp>"   the file must not match
    "<path>"               the path must exist
  """
  asserts: [String!]! = []
  """Names of the tests that demonstrate this document, for a human to follow."""
  tests: [String!]! = []
  """Ids of documents that should be composed alongside this one."""
  related: [String!]! = []
  """Ids of documents this one replaces."""
  supersedes: [String!]! = []
  """
  The newest commit touching `anchors` when this document was last reconciled
  with the code. Empty means never reconciled.
  """
  verifiedAt: String! = ""
  """Fold this document into the agent's system prompt at session start."""
  pin: Boolean! = false
}

"""
A loaded context document: its id, its front matter, and its markdown body.
"""
type Doc {
  id: String!
  meta: DocMeta!
  body: String!
}

enum DocKind { GLOSSARY FACT DECISION DESIGN NOTE }
enum DocStatus { DRAFT ACTIVE SUPERSEDED }

# How a document stands relative to the code it anchors to.
enum DriftState {
  # Its anchors have not moved since it was last reconciled.
  FRESH
  # Commits have touched its anchors since it was last reconciled.
  DRIFTED
  # It has anchors but has never been reconciled with them.
  UNVERIFIED
  # Its anchors have no committed history yet.
  UNCOMMITTED
  # It declares no anchors, so nothing ties it to the code.
  UNANCHORED
  # Its recorded commit is not in the window, or git could not be read.
  UNKNOWN
}

"""
The project's knowledge as documents: facts, decisions, designs and a
glossary, kept in the repository, composed into context on purpose, and
checked against the code that they describe.
"""
type Context {
  # Where the corpus lives, relative to the workspace root. A property of the
  # project, so it belongs in dagger.toml rather than in a tool argument:
  #
  #     [env.dev.modules.context.settings]
  #     root = "context"
  let root: String! = "context"

  # Whether `lint` insists that a `fact` carry evidence — an anchor, an
  # assertion or a named test. On by default: a fact nobody can check is a
  # rumour, and the point of the corpus is that it cannot fill up with those.
  let requireEvidence: Boolean! = true

  new(
    """
    Directory holding the corpus, relative to the workspace root.
    """
    root: String! = "context",
    """
    Require every `fact` document to declare an anchor, an assertion or a
    test, so nothing unverifiable accumulates.
    """
    requireEvidence: Boolean! = true,
  ) {
    self.root = root
    self.requireEvidence = requireEvidence
    self
  }

  """
  Compose the context-document tools onto a base LLM, and fold the corpus
  index — plus any pinned document — into the system prompt.
  """
  agent(base: LLM!): LLM! @agent {
    base
      # The three @check functions are excluded from the toolset on purpose.
      # They are already reachable the way every other check is, and a bare
      # `lint` tool is exactly the sort of general-purpose name that collides
      # with another module and forces EVERY tool of BOTH modules into
      # namespaced form (see context/facts/colliding-tool-names-...).
      .withTools(currentNode, except: ["lint", "assertions", "drifted"])
      .withSystemPrompt(systemPrompt(base.workspace))
  }

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- reading

  """
  List the context documents: one line each, grouped by kind, with how each
  one stands relative to the code it describes.

  This is the cheap "what does this project already know?" view. Read it
  before deriving anything from the source: a fact that is already written
  down costs one `docRead` instead of a dozen greps. Documents marked
  DRIFTED describe code that has changed since they were last reconciled —
  `docStale` says which commits moved them.
  """
  docs(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Only list documents of this kind: glossary, fact, decision, design, note.
    """
    kind: String! = "",
    """
    Only list documents carrying this tag.
    """
    tag: String! = "",
    """
    Only list documents in this state: draft, active, superseded. Empty
    lists everything except superseded documents.
    """
    status: String! = "",
    """
    Also print each document's anchors, tags and evidence.
    """
    detail: Boolean! = false,
  ): String! {
    let all = loadAll(ws)
    if (all.isEmpty) {
      return emptyCorpus
    }
    let selected = all.filter { d =>
      (kind == "" or d.meta.kind == kind) and
      (tag == "" or d.meta.tags.contains(tag)) and
      (if (status == "") { d.meta.status != "superseded" } else { d.meta.status == status })
    }
    if (selected.isEmpty) {
      return `No context document matches that filter. ${toString(all.length)} document(s) exist in ${root}/; call docs with no arguments to see them.`
    }

    let dirty = dirtyPaths(ws)
    let sections: [String!]! = []
    kindOrder.each { k =>
      let group = selected.filter { d => d.meta.kind == k }
      if (group.isEmpty) {
        continue
      }
      let rows = group.map { d => row(ws, d, dirty, detail) }
      sections += [k + "\n" + rows.join("\n")]
    }
    # Anything carrying a kind the corpus does not know about still has to be
    # listed, or `lint` would be the only place it ever shows up.
    let stray = selected.filter { d => !kindOrder.contains(d.meta.kind) }
    if (!stray.isEmpty) {
      sections += ["(unknown kind)\n" + stray.map { d => row(ws, d, dirty, detail) }.join("\n")]
    }

    banner(all) + "\n\n" + sections.join("\n\n") + "\n\n" + legend
  }

  """
  Read one or more context documents in full — front matter, body, and how
  the document stands relative to the code it anchors to.

  Documents are plain markdown files under the corpus directory, so the
  ordinary file tools work on them too; this renders the parsed form, with
  the links and the drift state a raw read would not show.
  """
  docRead(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Document ids, as printed by `docs` — the path under the corpus
    directory without the .md suffix, e.g. "facts/agent-composition".
    """
    ids: [String!]!,
  ): String! {
    if (ids.isEmpty) {
      raise "docRead needs at least one document id; `docs` lists them"
    }
    let dirty = dirtyPaths(ws)
    ids.map { id => renderDoc(ws, load(ws, id), dirty) }.join("\n\n" + separator + "\n\n")
  }

  """
  Search the corpus for a regular expression, in both the front matter and
  the prose, and report the matching documents with their matching lines.

  Reach for it when you do not know which document holds something —
  `docs` for the shape of the corpus, `docSearch` for a phrase, `docRead`
  once you know the id.
  """
  docSearch(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Regular expression to look for, matched case-insensitively.
    """
    pattern: String!,
    """
    Maximum number of matching lines to print per document.
    """
    perDoc: Int! = 4,
  ): String! {
    let all = loadAll(ws)
    if (all.isEmpty) {
      return emptyCorpus
    }
    let rx = (("(?i)" + pattern) :: Regexp!) rescue { e: Error =>
      raise `"${pattern}" is not a valid regular expression: ${plain(e.message)}`
    }
    let hits = all.map { d =>
      let lines = (d.body + "\n" + d.meta.title + "\n" + d.meta.tags.join(" "))
        .split("\n")
        .filter { line => line.containsMatch(rx) }
      {{doc: d, lines: lines}}
    }.filter { h => !h.lines.isEmpty }
    if (hits.isEmpty) {
      return `No context document matches /${pattern}/. ${toString(all.length)} document(s) searched in ${root}/.`
    }
    let blocks = hits.map { h =>
      let shown = h.lines.takeFirst(perDoc)
      let omitted = h.lines.length - shown.length
      let more = if (omitted > 0) { "\n    … " + toString(omitted) + " more matching line(s)" } else { "" }
      `${h.doc.id}  —  ${title(h.doc)}` + "\n" +
        shown.map { line => "    " + line.trimSpace }.join("\n") + more
    }
    `${toString(hits.length)} document(s) match /${pattern}/:` + "\n\n" + blocks.join("\n\n")
  }

  """
  Assemble a context bundle: the documents you select, plus the closure of
  everything they link to, ordered so definitions land before the things that
  use them, deduplicated, and clipped to a line budget.

  This is the deliberate alternative to letting a conversation accumulate:
  instead of hoping the right material drifted into the window, name what
  this task needs — by id, by tag, or by kind — and read the bundle. The
  manifest at the top says exactly what went in, what was left out for
  budget, and which of the included documents have drifted from the code, so
  nothing in your context is there without you knowing why.

  With no selection at all it composes every active document, which is the
  right move at the start of a task in an unfamiliar area.
  """
  docCompose(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Document ids to include. Superseded documents are included only when
    named here explicitly.
    """
    ids: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Include every active document carrying any of these tags.
    """
    tags: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Include every active document of any of these kinds: glossary, fact,
    decision, design, note.
    """
    kinds: [String!]! = [],
    """
    How many times to follow `related` links out from the selection. 0 takes
    the selection literally.
    """
    depth: Int! = 1,
    """
    Stop after roughly this many lines. Documents that do not fit are named
    in the manifest instead of being silently dropped.
    """
    maxLines: Int! = 1200,
  ): String! {
    let all = loadAll(ws)
    if (all.isEmpty) {
      return emptyCorpus
    }

    let named = ids.filter { id => all.any { d => d.id == id } }
    let missing = ids.filter { id => !all.any { d => d.id == id } }
    if (!missing.isEmpty) {
      raise `no such context document(s): ${missing.join(", ")} — call docs to see what exists`
    }
    let matched = all.filter { d =>
      d.meta.status != "superseded" and
      (tags.any { t => d.meta.tags.contains(t) } or kinds.contains(d.meta.kind))
    }.map { d => d.id }
    let seeds = if (named.isEmpty and matched.isEmpty and tags.isEmpty and kinds.isEmpty) {
      all.filter { d => d.meta.status == "active" }.map { d => d.id }
    } else {
      (named + matched).uniq
    }
    if (seeds.isEmpty) {
      return "That selection matches no context document. Call docs to see what the corpus holds."
    }

    # Follow `related` outward. Links are the corpus's own opinion about what
    # belongs together — a fact naming the design it came from, a design
    # naming the glossary entry its vocabulary depends on — so expanding them
    # is what turns a list of ids into a bundle that reads on its own.
    let selected = seeds
    let rounds = 0
    loop {
      if (rounds >= depth) {
        break
      }
      let links = all
        .filter { d => selected.contains(d.id) }
        .reduce([] :: [String!]!) { acc, d => acc + d.meta.related }
      let next = (selected + links.filter { r => all.any { c => c.id == r } }).uniq
      if (next.length == selected.length) {
        break
      }
      selected = next
      rounds += 1
    }
    # A superseded document reached through a link is noise, unless it was
    # asked for by name.
    let chosen = all.filter { d =>
      selected.contains(d.id) and (d.meta.status != "superseded" or ids.contains(d.id))
    }

    let ordered: [Doc!]! = []
    kindOrder.each { k =>
      ordered += chosen.filter { d => d.meta.kind == k }
    }
    ordered += chosen.filter { d => !kindOrder.contains(d.meta.kind) }

    let dirty = dirtyPaths(ws)
    let used = 0
    let included: [String!]! = []
    let omitted: [String!]! = []
    let warned: [String!]! = []
    let blocks: [String!]! = []
    ordered.each { d =>
      let block = composeBlock(d)
      let lines = block.split("\n").length
      # Always admit the first document, whatever the budget: a bundle that
      # is empty because one document is long is worse than one over budget.
      if (used + lines > maxLines and !included.isEmpty) {
        omitted += [d.id]
        continue
      }
      let state = driftOf(ws, d, dirty)
      if (state.state == DriftState.DRIFTED) {
        warned += [`${d.id} (${state.detail})`]
      }
      used += lines
      included += [d.id]
      blocks += [block]
    }

    let manifest: [String!]! = [
      `# Composed context — ${toString(included.length)} document(s), ~${toString(used)} lines, from ${root}/`,
      `# included: ${included.join(", ")}`,
    ]
    if (!omitted.isEmpty) {
      manifest += [`# omitted (line budget ${toString(maxLines)}): ${omitted.join(", ")} — raise maxLines or narrow the selection`]
    }
    if (!warned.isEmpty) {
      manifest += ["# DRIFTED — these describe code that has changed since they were verified; trust the code over them, and reconcile:"]
      manifest += warned.map { w => "#   " + w }
    }
    manifest.join("\n") + "\n\n" + blocks.join("\n\n" + separator + "\n\n")
  }

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- writing

  """
  Create or update a context document, and refuse to record one whose
  assertions do not hold.

  This is how a session leaves something behind. When you work out how a
  subsystem behaves, why an approach was rejected, or what a word means in
  this codebase, write it down here instead of leaving it in the
  conversation: the next session starts from the document, and the
  conversation is thrown away.

  Give the document evidence. `anchors` are the files it describes — they
  are what makes drift detectable later. `asserts` are claims about those
  files that a machine can re-check ("core/agents.go ~ func .*Compose"), and
  they are verified BEFORE the document is written, so a fact that is
  already wrong cannot be recorded. `tests` names the tests a human should
  read to believe it.

  Updating an existing document merges: arguments you leave empty keep
  whatever the document already had, so a small correction does not have to
  restate the whole front matter. The write lands in the workspace like any
  other edit — it shows up in `status`, and it is committed with the code it
  describes.
  """
  docWrite(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Document id: the path under the corpus directory, without the .md
    suffix, e.g. "facts/agent-composition". Use the conventional folders —
    glossary/, facts/, decisions/, designs/.
    """
    id: String!,
    """
    One-line title, in the present tense, stating the point of the document
    rather than its topic: "@agent middlewares fold alphabetically onto one
    LLM", not "Agent composition".
    """
    title: String! = "",
    """
    What sort of document this is.
    """
    kind: DocKind = null,
    """
    The document's prose, as markdown, without front matter and without
    repeating the title as a heading.
    """
    body: String! = "",
    """
    Labels used to select this document for composition, e.g. ["agents",
    "llm"].
    """
    tags: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Repository-relative files or directories this document describes.
    Changes to them are what makes it drift.
    """
    anchors: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Mechanically checkable claims: "<path> ~ <regexp>" to require a match,
    "<path> !~ <regexp>" to forbid one, or a bare "<path>" to require the
    path to exist. Checked before the write, and by the context:assertions
    check thereafter.
    """
    asserts: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Names of the tests that demonstrate this, e.g.
    ["AgentsSuite/TestComposeToolset"].
    """
    tests: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Ids of documents that should be composed alongside this one.
    """
    related: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Ids of documents this one replaces. Mark those superseded as well.
    """
    supersedes: [String!]! = [],
    """
    Lifecycle state. Defaults to active for a new document, and to whatever
    it already was for an existing one.
    """
    status: DocStatus = null,
    """
    Fold this document into every session's system prompt. Reserve it for
    the few documents that everything else assumes.
    """
    pin: Boolean = null,
  ): Workspace! {
    validateId(id)
    let priorText = ws.file(docPath(id)).contents rescue { e: Error => "" }
    let isNew = priorText == ""
    let prior = if (isNew) { DocMeta(title: "") } else { parse(id, priorText).meta }

    let meta = DocMeta(
      title: if (title == "") { prior.title } else { title },
      kind: if (kind == null) { if (isNew) { "note" } else { prior.kind } } else { kindName(kind ?? DocKind.NOTE) },
      status: if (status == null) { if (isNew) { "active" } else { prior.status } } else { statusName(status ?? DocStatus.ACTIVE) },
      tags: if (tags.isEmpty) { prior.tags } else { tags },
      anchors: if (anchors.isEmpty) { prior.anchors } else { anchors },
      asserts: if (asserts.isEmpty) { prior.asserts } else { asserts },
      tests: if (tests.isEmpty) { prior.tests } else { tests },
      related: if (related.isEmpty) { prior.related } else { related },
      supersedes: if (supersedes.isEmpty) { prior.supersedes } else { supersedes },
      pin: pin ?? prior.pin,
    )
    let text = if (body == "") {
      if (isNew) {
        raise `${id} is a new document, so it needs a body`
      }
      parse(id, priorText).body
    } else {
      body.trimSpace + "\n"
    }

    let missingAnchors = meta.anchors.filter { a => !ws.directory("/").exists(a.trimPrefix("/")) }
    if (!missingAnchors.isEmpty) {
      raise `${id}: anchor(s) do not exist in the workspace: ${missingAnchors.join(", ")}`
    }
    let failures = meta.asserts.map { a => runAssert(ws, a) }.filter { f => f != "" }
    if (!failures.isEmpty) {
      raise `${id}: refusing to write — ${toString(failures.length)} assertion(s) do not hold:` + "\n" +
        failures.map { f => "  - " + f }.join("\n") +
        "\n\nEither the document is wrong, or the assertion is. Read the anchored code before insisting."
    }

    let stamped = stamp(meta, newestAnchorCommit(ws, meta.anchors))
    let verb = if (isNew) { "Wrote" } else { "Updated" }
    # Writing a document re-stamps it, because writing it IS reconciling it
    # with the code as it stands. Say so out loud when that clears a drift:
    # laundering a drifted document with a cosmetic edit should be visible in
    # the trace, not silent.
    let moved = !isNew and prior.verifiedAt != "" and prior.verifiedAt != stamped.verifiedAt
    let note = if (moved) {
      ` — re-verified ${shortSha(prior.verifiedAt)} -> ${shortSha(stamped.verifiedAt)}`
    } else {
      stampNote(stamped)
    }
    print(`${verb} ${docPath(id)} — ${stamped.kind}/${stamped.status}` + note)
    ws.withNewFile(docPath(id), render(stamped, text))
  }

  """
  Re-stamp documents as reconciled with the code they anchor to.

  Do this after reading a drifted document against the code that moved and
  either correcting it or confirming it still holds. Assertions are re-run
  first, so re-stamping is a claim you have to be able to back: a document
  whose assertions fail cannot be verified, only fixed.
  """
  docVerify(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Document ids to re-stamp. Empty re-stamps every drifted document, which
    is only honest if you have just read all of them.
    """
    ids: [String!]! = [],
  ): Workspace! {
    let dirty = dirtyPaths(ws)
    let targets = if (ids.isEmpty) {
      loadAll(ws).filter { d => driftOf(ws, d, dirty).state == DriftState.DRIFTED }.map { d => d.id }
    } else {
      ids
    }
    if (targets.isEmpty) {
      print("Nothing to verify: no document has drifted.")
      return ws
    }
    let out = ws
    targets.each { id =>
      let doc = load(out, id)
      let failures = doc.meta.asserts.map { a => runAssert(out, a) }.filter { f => f != "" }
      if (!failures.isEmpty) {
        raise `${id}: cannot verify — ${toString(failures.length)} assertion(s) do not hold:` + "\n" +
          failures.map { f => "  - " + f }.join("\n")
      }
      let was = doc.meta.verifiedAt
      let meta = stamp(doc.meta, newestAnchorCommit(out, doc.meta.anchors))
      if (meta.verifiedAt == was) {
        print(`${id}: already verified at ${shortSha(was)}; unchanged`)
        continue
      }
      print(`${id}: verified ${shortSha(was)} -> ${shortSha(meta.verifiedAt)}`)
      out = out.withNewFile(docPath(id), render(meta, doc.body))
    }
    out
  }

  """
  Report how the corpus stands relative to the code: which documents are
  still in step with their anchors, and for the ones that are not, exactly
  which commits moved underneath them.

  This is the reconciliation worklist. For each drifted document, read the
  named commits, then correct the document with `docWrite` — or, if it still
  holds as written, confirm it with `docVerify`.
  """
  docStale(
    ws: Workspace!,
    """
    Document ids to report on. Empty reports the whole corpus.
    """
    ids: [String!]! = [],
  ): String! {
    let all = loadAll(ws)
    if (all.isEmpty) {
      return emptyCorpus
    }
    let selected = if (ids.isEmpty) { all } else { ids.map { id => load(ws, id) } }
    let dirty = dirtyPaths(ws)
    # One drift computation per document: each one walks git history, so the
    # listing and the count below share the result rather than asking twice.
    let states = selected.map { d => {{doc: d, state: driftOf(ws, d, dirty)}} }
    let rows = states.map { s =>
      `${stateTag(s.state.state)}  ${s.doc.id}` + "\n    " + s.state.detail
    }
    let drifted = states.filter { s => s.state.state == DriftState.DRIFTED }
    let footer = if (drifted.isEmpty) {
      "Nothing has drifted."
    } else {
      `${toString(drifted.length)} document(s) have drifted. Read the commits named above, then correct them ` +
        "with docWrite, or confirm and re-stamp them with docVerify."
    }
    rows.join("\n") + "\n\n" + footer
  }

  # ----------------------------------------------------------------- checks

  """
  Check that every context document is well formed: front matter that
  parses, a known kind and status, links that resolve, anchors that exist,
  and — for facts — evidence that somebody could check.
  """
  lint(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let files = paths(ws)
    if (files.isEmpty) {
      print(`No context documents in ${root}/ yet.`)
      return null
    }
    let ids = files.map { p => idOf(p) }
    let errors: [String!]! = []
    files.each { p =>
      errors += lintOne(ws, p, ids)
    }
    # The supersession cross-check needs every document parsed, so it only
    # runs once the per-document pass came back clean.
    if (errors.isEmpty) {
      let all = loadAll(ws)
      all.each { d =>
        errors += all
          .filter { x => d.meta.supersedes.contains(x.id) and x.meta.status != "superseded" }
          .map { x => `${d.id} supersedes ${x.id}, but ${x.id} is still marked "${x.meta.status}"` }
      }
    }
    if (!errors.isEmpty) {
      raise `${toString(errors.length)} problem(s) in ${root}/:` + "\n" + errors.map { e => "  - " + e }.join("\n")
    }
    print(`${toString(files.length)} context document(s) in ${root}/ are well formed.`)
    null
  }

  """
  Check that every assertion recorded in the corpus still holds against the
  code as it stands.

  This is the half of convergence that needs no judgement: a document said
  the code says something, and either it still does or it does not.
  """
  assertions(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let all = loadAll(ws)
    let checked = 0
    let failures: [String!]! = []
    all.each { d =>
      checked += d.meta.asserts.length
      failures += d.meta.asserts
        .map { a =>
          let why = runAssert(ws, a)
          if (why == "") { "" } else { `${d.id}: ${why}` }
        }
        .filter { f => f != "" }
    }
    if (!failures.isEmpty) {
      raise `${toString(failures.length)} of ${toString(checked)} assertion(s) no longer hold:` + "\n" +
        failures.map { f => "  - " + f }.join("\n") +
        "\n\nRead the anchored code, correct the document with docWrite, then re-stamp it with docVerify."
    }
    print(`${toString(checked)} assertion(s) across ${toString(all.length)} document(s) still hold.`)
    null
  }

  """
  Check that no active document describes code that has moved since the
  document was last reconciled with it.

  This is the loop that keeps the corpus converging: changing anchored code
  is what turns a document red, and the only way back to green is to read
  the document against the change and either fix it or confirm it. Drafts
  are exempt — they are work in progress by definition.
  """
  drifted(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let all = loadAll(ws).filter { d => d.meta.status == "active" }
    if (all.isEmpty) {
      print(`No active context documents in ${root}/.`)
      return null
    }
    let dirty = dirtyPaths(ws)
    let bad: [String!]! = []
    let notes: [String!]! = []
    let fresh = 0
    all.each { d =>
      let state = driftOf(ws, d, dirty)
      # Every arm ends in null so the case stays one type: it is used for its
      # effect on the accumulators, not for a value.
      case (state.state) {
        DriftState.DRIFTED => {
          bad += [`${d.id}: ${state.detail}`]
          null
        }

        DriftState.UNVERIFIED => {
          bad += [`${d.id}: ${state.detail} — run docVerify after reading it against ${d.meta.anchors.join(", ")}`]
          null
        }

        DriftState.FRESH => {
          fresh += 1
          null
        }

        else => {
          notes += [`${d.id}: ${stateName(state.state)} — ${state.detail}`]
          null
        }
      }
    }
    if (!notes.isEmpty) {
      print("Not checked:\n" + notes.map { n => "  - " + n }.join("\n"))
    }
    if (!bad.isEmpty) {
      raise `${toString(bad.length)} document(s) have drifted from the code they describe:` + "\n" +
        bad.map { b => "  - " + b }.join("\n") +
        "\n\nRead the named commits, then correct the document with docWrite, or confirm and re-stamp it with docVerify."
    }
    print(`${toString(fresh)} active document(s) are in step with the code they describe.`)
    null
  }

  # ---------------------------------------------------------------- corpus

  # The order documents are composed in: what words mean, then what is true,
  # then what was decided, then where it is going. A design that leans on a
  # fact reads correctly only if the fact came first.
  let kindOrder: [String!]! = ["glossary", "fact", "decision", "design", "note"]

  let statusNames: [String!]! = ["draft", "active", "superseded"]

  # How far back to look for the commit a document was verified at. Past
  # this, drift can still be seen but not attributed, and the document is
  # reported UNKNOWN rather than guessed at.
  let driftWindow: Int! = 200

  let separator: String! = "---"

  let emptyCorpus: String! =
    "This project has no context documents yet. Start one with docWrite — a glossary entry for a " +
    "word this codebase uses in its own way, or a fact you had to dig for and would rather not dig " +
    "for again."

  let legend: String! =
    "Legend: [!] drifted from the code it describes · [?] unverified · [~] not anchored to any code. " +
    "docStale explains a marked document; docRead reads one; docCompose assembles a bundle."

  let docPath(id: String!): String! {
    "/" + root + "/" + id + ".md"
  }

  let idOf(relative: String!): String! {
    relative.trimSuffix(".md")
  }

  # Two globs, because a "**/" prefix is not guaranteed to match at depth
  # zero: the corpus keeps its conventional folders, but a document sitting
  # directly in the root has to be found too.
  let paths(ws: Workspace!): [String!]! {
    let dir = ws.directory("/" + root)
    (dir.glob("*.md") + dir.glob("**/*.md")).uniq rescue { e: Error => [] :: [String!]! }
  }

  let readRaw(ws: Workspace!, relative: String!): String! {
    ws.file("/" + root + "/" + relative).contents
  }

  let load(ws: Workspace!, id: String!): Doc! {
    let raw = ws.file(docPath(id)).contents rescue { e: Error =>
      raise `no context document "${id}" (looked for ${docPath(id)}) — call docs to see what exists`
    }
    parse(id, raw)
  }

  let loadAll(ws: Workspace!): [Doc!]! {
    paths(ws).map { p => parse(idOf(p), readRaw(ws, p)) }
  }

  # A document is a YAML front-matter block, a "---" line, and markdown. The
  # format is deliberately the one every static site generator already uses:
  # the files stay readable and editable by hand, which is the whole point of
  # keeping them in the repository rather than in a database.
  let parse(id: String!, raw: String!): Doc! {
    if (!raw.hasPrefix("---\n")) {
      raise `${id}: no front matter — the file must begin with a "---" line`
    }
    let parts = raw.trimPrefix("---\n").split("\n---\n", 2)
    if (parts.length < 2) {
      raise `${id}: front matter is not closed by a "---" line`
    }
    let meta = decodeMeta(parts[0] ?? "") rescue { e: Error =>
      raise `${id}: front matter is not valid YAML: ${plain(e.message)}`
    }
    Doc(id: id, meta: meta, body: (parts[1] ?? "").trim("\n"))
  }

  # Type-directed: the declared return type is what tells YAML.decode what to
  # build, so this stays a bare one-liner.
  let decodeMeta(front: String!): DocMeta! {
    YAML.decode(front)
  }

  let title(doc: Doc!): String! {
    if (doc.meta.title.trimSpace == "") { doc.id } else { doc.meta.title.trimSpace }
  }

  let validateId(id: String!): String! {
    if (!id.containsMatch(`^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9._/-]*$`)) {
      raise `"${id}" is not a usable document id: lowercase letters, digits, "." "-" "_" and "/" only, e.g. "facts/agent-composition"`
    }
    if (id.contains("..") or id.hasSuffix("/") or id.hasSuffix(".md")) {
      raise `"${id}" is not a usable document id: no "..", no trailing "/", and no ".md" suffix`
    }
    id
  }

  # --------------------------------------------------------------- evidence

  let dirtyPaths(ws: Workspace!): [String!]! {
    ws.git.uncommitted.diffStats.{{path}}.map { s => s.path } rescue { e: Error => [] :: [String!]! }
  }

  # The newest commit touching these anchors, which is what "verified at"
  # records. Comparing one sha is all drift detection needs: if the newest
  # commit touching the anchors is still the one the document was reconciled
  # against, nothing has moved underneath it.
  let newestAnchorCommit(ws: Workspace!, anchors: [String!]!): String! {
    if (anchors.isEmpty) {
      return ""
    }
    let shas = ws.git.head.log(limit: 1, paths: anchors).{{sha}}.map { c => c.sha } rescue { e: Error => [] :: [String!]! }
    shas.takeFirst.join("")
  }

  let driftOf(ws: Workspace!, doc: Doc!, dirty: [String!]!): {{state: DriftState!, detail: String!}}! {
    let anchors = doc.meta.anchors
    if (anchors.isEmpty) {
      return {{state: DriftState.UNANCHORED, detail: "declares no anchors, so nothing ties it to the code"}}
    }
    let touched = dirty.filter { p => anchors.any { a => Path(a).contains(Path(p)) } }
    let note = if (touched.isEmpty) {
      ""
    } else {
      ` — and there are uncommitted edits to ${touched.takeFirst(4).join(", ")}`
    }
    let recent = ws.git.head.log(limit: driftWindow, paths: anchors).{{sha, shortSha, message}} rescue { e: Error =>
      return {{state: DriftState.UNKNOWN, detail: `cannot read git history: ${plain(e.message)}`}}
    }
    if (recent.isEmpty) {
      return {{state: DriftState.UNCOMMITTED, detail: "its anchors have no committed history yet" + note}}
    }
    if (doc.meta.verifiedAt == "") {
      return {{state: DriftState.UNVERIFIED, detail: `never reconciled with ${anchors.join(", ")}`}}
    }
    let since = recent.takeWhile { c => !c.sha.hasPrefix(doc.meta.verifiedAt) }
    if (since.length == recent.length) {
      return {{
        state: DriftState.UNKNOWN,
        detail: `its verifiedAt ${shortSha(doc.meta.verifiedAt)} is not among the last ` +
          `${toString(driftWindow)} commit(s) touching its anchors — re-read it and re-verify`,
      }}
    }
    if (since.isEmpty) {
      return {{state: DriftState.FRESH, detail: `in step with ${anchors.join(", ")}` + note}}
    }
    {{
      state: DriftState.DRIFTED,
      detail: `${toString(since.length)} commit(s) touched ${anchors.join(", ")} since it was verified: ` +
        since.takeFirst(5).map { c => `${c.shortSha} ${subject(c.message)}` }.join("; ") + note,
    }}
  }

  # Returns "" when the assertion holds, or a one-line reason when it does not.
  let runAssert(ws: Workspace!, spec: String!): String! {
    let text = spec.trimSpace
    if (text == "") {
      return ""
    }
    let negated = text.contains(" !~ ")
    let sep = if (negated) { " !~ " } else if (text.contains(" ~ ")) { " ~ " } else { "" }
    if (sep == "") {
      # A bare path is an existence claim, which is the weakest useful
      # assertion: the thing this document is about is still there. Anything
      # with a space or a stray tilde in it is a mistyped operator, not a
      # path, and saying so beats reporting a missing file.
      if (text.containsMatch(`[\s~]`)) {
        return malformedAssert(text)
      }
      let there = ws.directory("/").exists(text.trimPrefix("/")) rescue { e: Error => false }
      return if (there) { "" } else { `${text} does not exist` }
    }
    let parts = text.split(sep, 2)
    let path = (parts[0] ?? "").trimSpace
    let pattern = (parts[1] ?? "").trimSpace
    let contents = ws.file("/" + path.trimPrefix("/")).contents rescue { e: Error =>
      return `${path} cannot be read (${plain(e.message)})`
    }
    let hit = contents.containsMatch((pattern :: Regexp!)) rescue { e: Error =>
      return `"${text}" is not a valid assertion: ${plain(e.message)}`
    }
    if (negated and hit) {
      return `${path} matches /${pattern}/, which this document says it must not`
    }
    if (!negated and !hit) {
      return `${path} no longer matches /${pattern}/`
    }
    ""
  }

  let assertShape(id: String!, spec: String!): [String!]! {
    let text = spec.trimSpace
    if (text == "") {
      return [`${id}: empty assertion`]
    }
    let sep = if (text.contains(" !~ ")) { " !~ " } else if (text.contains(" ~ ")) { " ~ " } else { "" }
    if (sep == "") {
      if (text.containsMatch(`[\s~]`)) {
        return [`${id}: ` + malformedAssert(text)]
      }
      return [] :: [String!]!
    }
    let parts = text.split(sep, 2)
    if ((parts[0] ?? "").trimSpace == "" or (parts[1] ?? "").trimSpace == "") {
      return [`${id}: ` + malformedAssert(text)]
    }
    [] :: [String!]!
  }

  let malformedAssert(text: String!): String! {
    `malformed assertion "${text}" — expected "<path>", "<path> ~ <regexp>" or "<path> !~ <regexp>"`
  }

  let lintOne(ws: Workspace!, relative: String!, ids: [String!]!): [String!]! {
    let id = idOf(relative)
    let doc = parse(id, readRaw(ws, relative)) rescue { e: Error =>
      return [plain(e.message)]
    }
    let meta = doc.meta
    let errs: [String!]! = []
    if (meta.title.trimSpace == "") {
      errs += [`${id}: no title`]
    }
    if (!kindOrder.contains(meta.kind)) {
      errs += [`${id}: unknown kind "${meta.kind}" (expected one of: ${kindOrder.join(", ")})`]
    }
    if (!statusNames.contains(meta.status)) {
      errs += [`${id}: unknown status "${meta.status}" (expected one of: ${statusNames.join(", ")})`]
    }
    if (doc.body.trimSpace == "") {
      errs += [`${id}: empty body`]
    }
    meta.related.each { r =>
      if (!ids.contains(r)) {
        errs += [`${id}: related document "${r}" does not exist`]
      }
    }
    meta.supersedes.each { s =>
      if (!ids.contains(s)) {
        errs += [`${id}: superseded document "${s}" does not exist`]
      }
    }
    meta.anchors.each { a =>
      let there = ws.directory("/").exists(a.trimPrefix("/")) rescue { e: Error => false }
      if (!there) {
        errs += [`${id}: anchor "${a}" does not exist`]
      }
    }
    meta.asserts.each { a =>
      errs += assertShape(id, a)
    }
    if (meta.pin and meta.status != "active") {
      errs += [`${id}: pinned, but its status is "${meta.status}" — only active documents belong in every session's prompt`]
    }
    if (requireEvidence and meta.kind == "fact" and meta.anchors.isEmpty and meta.asserts.isEmpty and meta.tests.isEmpty) {
      errs += [`${id}: a fact needs evidence — an anchor, an assertion, or a named test`]
    }
    errs
  }

  # -------------------------------------------------------------- rendering

  let banner(all: [Doc!]!): String! {
    let active = all.filter { d => d.meta.status == "active" }.length
    let draft = all.filter { d => d.meta.status == "draft" }.length
    let superseded = all.filter { d => d.meta.status == "superseded" }.length
    `${root}/ — ${toString(all.length)} document(s): ${toString(active)} active, ` +
      `${toString(draft)} draft, ${toString(superseded)} superseded`
  }

  let row(ws: Workspace!, doc: Doc!, dirty: [String!]!, detail: Boolean!): String! {
    let state = driftOf(ws, doc, dirty)
    let head = "  " + stateTag(state.state) + " " + doc.id.padRight(38) + " " +
      doc.meta.status.padRight(11) + " " + title(doc)
    if (!detail) {
      return head
    }
    let bits: [String!]! = []
    if (!doc.meta.tags.isEmpty) {
      bits += ["tags: " + doc.meta.tags.join(", ")]
    }
    if (!doc.meta.anchors.isEmpty) {
      bits += ["anchors: " + doc.meta.anchors.join(", ")]
    }
    if (!doc.meta.asserts.isEmpty) {
      bits += [toString(doc.meta.asserts.length) + " assertion(s)"]
    }
    if (!doc.meta.tests.isEmpty) {
      bits += ["tests: " + doc.meta.tests.join(", ")]
    }
    if (!doc.meta.related.isEmpty) {
      bits += ["related: " + doc.meta.related.join(", ")]
    }
    if (bits.isEmpty) {
      head
    } else {
      head + "\n      " + bits.join(" · ")
    }
  }

  let renderDoc(ws: Workspace!, doc: Doc!, dirty: [String!]!): String! {
    let state = driftOf(ws, doc, dirty)
    let meta = doc.meta
    let bits: [String!]! = [`kind: ${meta.kind}`, `status: ${meta.status}`]
    if (!meta.tags.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`tags: ${meta.tags.join(", ")}`]
    }
    if (!meta.anchors.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`anchors: ${meta.anchors.join(", ")}`]
    }
    if (!meta.tests.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`tests: ${meta.tests.join(", ")}`]
    }
    if (!meta.related.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`related: ${meta.related.join(", ")}`]
    }
    if (!meta.supersedes.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`supersedes: ${meta.supersedes.join(", ")}`]
    }
    let assertBlock = if (meta.asserts.isEmpty) {
      ""
    } else {
      "\nassertions:\n" + meta.asserts.map { a => "  " + a }.join("\n")
    }
    `# ${doc.id} — ${title(doc)}` + "\n" +
      bits.join("\n") + assertBlock + "\n" +
      `drift: ${stateName(state.state)} — ${state.detail}` + "\n\n" +
      doc.body
  }

  let composeBlock(doc: Doc!): String! {
    let bits: [String!]! = [`kind: ${doc.meta.kind}`]
    if (!doc.meta.anchors.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`anchors: ${doc.meta.anchors.join(", ")}`]
    }
    if (!doc.meta.tests.isEmpty) {
      bits += [`tests: ${doc.meta.tests.join(", ")}`]
    }
    if (doc.meta.verifiedAt != "") {
      bits += [`verified at ${shortSha(doc.meta.verifiedAt)}`]
    }
    `## ${doc.id} — ${title(doc)}` + "\n" +
      `<!-- ${bits.join(" · ")} -->` + "\n\n" +
      doc.body.trimSpace
  }

  # A document on disk: front matter, a closing rule, then the prose. Fields
  # that carry nothing are omitted rather than written empty, so a
  # hand-written document and a generated one look the same.
  let render(meta: DocMeta!, body: String!): String! {
    "---\n" + frontMatter(meta) + "---\n\n" + body.trimSpace + "\n"
  }

  let frontMatter(meta: DocMeta!): String! {
    let out = "title: " + yamlString(meta.title) + "\n"
    out += "kind: " + meta.kind + "\n"
    out += "status: " + meta.status + "\n"
    if (!meta.tags.isEmpty) {
      out += "tags: " + yamlList(meta.tags) + "\n"
    }
    if (!meta.anchors.isEmpty) {
      out += "anchors: " + yamlList(meta.anchors) + "\n"
    }
    if (!meta.asserts.isEmpty) {
      out += "asserts: " + yamlList(meta.asserts) + "\n"
    }
    if (!meta.tests.isEmpty) {
      out += "tests: " + yamlList(meta.tests) + "\n"
    }
    if (!meta.related.isEmpty) {
      out += "related: " + yamlList(meta.related) + "\n"
    }
    if (!meta.supersedes.isEmpty) {
      out += "supersedes: " + yamlList(meta.supersedes) + "\n"
    }
    if (meta.verifiedAt != "") {
      out += "verifiedAt: " + meta.verifiedAt + "\n"
    }
    if (meta.pin) {
      out += "pin: true\n"
    }
    out
  }

  let yamlString(s: String!): String! {
    "\"" + s.replace("\\", "\\\\").replace("\"", "\\\"").replace("\n", "\\n").replace("\t", "\\t") + "\""
  }

  let yamlList(xs: [String!]!): String! {
    "[" + xs.map { x => yamlString(x) }.join(", ") + "]"
  }

  # DocMeta is a value; re-stamping builds a new one rather than mutating the
  # loaded document.
  let stamp(meta: DocMeta!, sha: String!): DocMeta! {
    DocMeta(
      title: meta.title,
      kind: meta.kind,
      status: meta.status,
      tags: meta.tags,
      anchors: meta.anchors,
      asserts: meta.asserts,
      tests: meta.tests,
      related: meta.related,
      supersedes: meta.supersedes,
      verifiedAt: sha,
      pin: meta.pin,
    )
  }

  let stampNote(meta: DocMeta!): String! {
    if (meta.anchors.isEmpty) {
      " — no anchors, so nothing will tell you when this stops being true"
    } else if (meta.verifiedAt == "") {
      " — its anchors have no committed history yet"
    } else {
      ` — verified at ${shortSha(meta.verifiedAt)}`
    }
  }

  let kindName(kind: DocKind!): String! {
    case (kind) {
      DocKind.GLOSSARY => "glossary"
      DocKind.FACT => "fact"
      DocKind.DECISION => "decision"
      DocKind.DESIGN => "design"
      DocKind.NOTE => "note"
      # An exhaustive case still infers a nullable result, so every one of
      # these needs a total fallback to stay String!.
      else => "note"
    }
  }

  let statusName(status: DocStatus!): String! {
    case (status) {
      DocStatus.DRAFT => "draft"
      DocStatus.ACTIVE => "active"
      DocStatus.SUPERSEDED => "superseded"
      else => "active"
    }
  }

  let stateName(state: DriftState!): String! {
    case (state) {
      DriftState.FRESH => "fresh"
      DriftState.DRIFTED => "drifted"
      DriftState.UNVERIFIED => "unverified"
      DriftState.UNCOMMITTED => "uncommitted"
      DriftState.UNANCHORED => "unanchored"
      DriftState.UNKNOWN => "unknown"
      else => "unknown"
    }
  }

  let stateTag(state: DriftState!): String! {
    case (state) {
      DriftState.FRESH => "   "
      DriftState.DRIFTED => "[!]"
      DriftState.UNVERIFIED => "[?]"
      DriftState.UNCOMMITTED => "[?]"
      DriftState.UNANCHORED => "[~]"
      DriftState.UNKNOWN => "[?]"
      else => "[?]"
    }
  }

  let subject(message: String!): String! {
    message.split("\n", 2).takeFirst.join("")
  }

  let shortSha(sha: String!): String! {
    if (sha == "") { "(never)" } else { sha.split("", 8).takeFirst(7).join("") }
  }

  # Engine errors carry a "[traceparent:...]" marker, which is an invitation
  # to go read a span over what is almost always a typo in a document.
  let plain(message: String!): String! {
    message.replaceMatches(`\s*\[traceparent:[^\]]*\]`, with: "").trimSpace
  }

  # ---------------------------------------------------------- system prompt

  let systemPrompt(ws: Workspace!): String! {
    # A corpus that will not parse must never break agent composition: say so
    # in the prompt and let context:lint be the place that explains why.
    let all = loadAll(ws) rescue { e: Error =>
      return corpusPrompt + "\n\nThe corpus in " + root + "/ could not be read (" +
        plain(e.message) + "). Fix it before relying on it; context:lint says what is wrong."
    }
    if (all.isEmpty) {
      return noCorpusPrompt
    }
    let listed = all.filter { d => d.meta.status != "superseded" }
    let index = listed.map { d => `- ${d.id} (${d.meta.kind}) — ${title(d)}` }
    let pinned = all.filter { d => d.meta.pin and d.meta.status == "active" }
    let pinnedBlock = if (pinned.isEmpty) {
      ""
    } else {
      "\n\nThese documents are pinned — they are standing context for this project, " +
        "and they are reproduced here in full so you do not have to fetch them:\n\n" +
        pinned.map { d => composeBlock(d) }.join("\n\n")
    }
    corpusPrompt + "\n\n" +
      `The corpus in ${root}/ holds ${toString(listed.length)} document(s):` + "\n\n" +
      index.join("\n") + pinnedBlock
  }

  let corpusPrompt: String! = """
    ## Context documents

    This project keeps what it knows in documents, not in conversation
    history. They live in the repository, they are composed into context on
    purpose, and they are checked against the code they describe. Your tools
    for them are `docs`, `docRead`, `docSearch`, `docCompose`, `docWrite`,
    `docStale` and `docVerify`.

    Read before you derive. When a task touches an area with documents,
    `docCompose` the relevant ones first — that is cheaper and more reliable
    than re-deriving from the source, and it is how you inherit reasoning
    nobody wrote down twice. A document marked DRIFTED describes code that
    has changed since it was last reconciled: trust the code over it, and say
    so.

    Write before you finish. A session that worked something out and left it
    in the chat has thrown it away. When you have established how a subsystem
    behaves, why an approach was rejected, or what a word means here,
    `docWrite` it: a `fact` for something small and checkable, a `decision`
    for a choice and the reasons against the alternatives, a `design` for
    where something is going, a `glossary` entry for a word this codebase
    uses in its own way. Keep documents short and separate — one claim per
    document, linked with `related` — because composition is per-document.

    Give every document evidence. `anchors` are the files it describes, and
    they are what makes drift detectable; `asserts` are claims a machine can
    re-check, and they are verified before the write goes through; `tests`
    names the tests that demonstrate it. A fact with no evidence is a rumour,
    and `context:lint` will reject it.

    Reconcile what you break. If you change code that documents anchor to,
    those documents drift, and `context:drifted` fails until somebody has
    read them against the change. Do it in the same session, not later: fix
    the document with `docWrite`, or confirm and re-stamp it with
    `docVerify`. Never re-stamp a document you have not just read against the
    code that moved.
  """

  let noCorpusPrompt: String! = """
    ## Context documents

    This project keeps what it knows in documents under the corpus directory,
    rather than in conversation history — but there are none yet. When this
    session establishes something worth keeping (how a subsystem behaves, why
    an approach was rejected, what a word means here), write it down with
    `docWrite` instead of leaving it in the chat: give it `anchors` (the files
    it describes) and `asserts` (claims a machine can re-check), so the next
    session can trust it and the checks can tell you when it stops being true.
  """
}
